<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.Map</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Harness.Map</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The coordinator's derivation half: the register map, the copy layer generated from it, and the
    non-retentive assertion both must satisfy. Pure arithmetic and text — no packages, no project
    references, no network, no Portal, no Siemens.Engineering.

    That is deliberate and it is what makes phase 2 cheap to verify: every refusal in here is a
    DESIGN-TIME refusal (X-A: "refused at MAP-DERIVATION time ... a design-time error, never a
    runtime one"), so the whole component is testable with no device, no rig and no TIA session.
  -->

  <!--
    The member-line grammar reader (IrMemberLine.cs) is internal: it is a SECOND copy of a grammar the
    converter owns, kept internal so nothing outside this assembly comes to depend on the copy. Its own
    tests must still be able to reach it, because the one failure a duplicated parser must not have is
    projecting a line WRONG rather than refusing it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Harness.Map.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
